    <item>
      <title>Filming the Next Move in Movement Disorders</title>
      <link>https://prisma.groningen/projects/movement_disorders/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 10 Jun 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>https://prisma.groningen/projects/movement_disorders/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 class=&#34;relative group&#34;&gt;Overview 
    &lt;div id=&#34;overview&#34; class=&#34;anchor&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
    
    &lt;span
        class=&#34;absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100&#34;&gt;
        &lt;a class=&#34;group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700 !no-underline&#34; href=&#34;#overview&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;/span&gt;        
    
&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Every day, you move. Cycling, typing on a keyboard, playing an instrument &amp;ndash; all of it is controlled by a  finely-tuned process that your brain, nerves, and muscles follow in order to translate your intentions into accurate, fine movements. This multi-system process doesn&amp;rsquo;t always go perfectly, however: Maybe you have experienced shaky hands or legs after a workout, or an annoying bout of hiccups.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://prisma.groningen/projects/movement_disorders/featuredleap.webp" />
    </item>
    
