    <item>
      <title>About page</title>
      <link>https://prisma.groningen/help/about/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 01 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>https://prisma.groningen/help/about/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;About contains general information about PRISMA, and links to the involved authors and author pages. You can create your own author page by adding a folder with your name, a picture with the name &amp;ldquo;featured_your_name&amp;rdquo; inside it, and a index.md page of your liking. Tag it with the &amp;lsquo;authors&amp;rsquo; tag so it appears in searches correctly.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once you do, you can edit the &amp;lsquo;hugo.toml&amp;rsquo; configuration in the main folder to add navigation from the About Us submenu to your page. Use the same format as the one for George:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      
    </item>
    
    <item>
      <title>Authors data</title>
      <link>https://prisma.groningen/help/authors/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 01 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>https://prisma.groningen/help/authors/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Authors are handled a bit differently than a normal page. While each researcher can have (and I would encourage them to have) a separate page for themselves under the /about/ page, the actual author header, image, socials, and description is kept under the /data/authors/ &lt;strong&gt;folder&lt;/strong&gt; in separate .jsons.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can modify any part of your own entry to your liking, though I&amp;rsquo;d encourage keeping things relatively uniform to the others. You can upload your image to the /assets/img/team/ folder. After that, you can modify or create a .json for yourself:&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      
    </item>
    
    <item>
      <title>George Azzopardi</title>
      <link>https://prisma.groningen/about/george/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 01 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>https://prisma.groningen/about/george/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;My name is George Azzopardi, and I am an Associate Professor in Pattern Recognition, embedded in the Information Systems group of the Bernoulli Institute for Mathematics, Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence, Faculty of Science and Engineering, University of Groningen, the Netherlands.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As the academic lead of PRISMA, I guide our research team in advancing the robustness of vision models, developing innovative approaches to machine learning, and transforming complex data into actionable insights.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
